          %title Noncommutative schemes
          %h1#noncommutative_schemes Noncommutative schemes
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#formal_frameworks"} Formal frameworks
              %li
                %a{:href => "#rosenbergs_definition"} Rosenberg’s definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#nonexamples"} Nonexamples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#literature"} Literature
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            One would like to have a proper extension of a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            which should include also noncommutative spaces represented by noncommutative rings (“noncommutative affine schemes”) and “noncommutative projective schemes” represented by graded rings, but
            %em not
            including commutative spaces which are not schemes (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stacks"}> stacks
            ,
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+space"} algebraic space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+topology"} Zariski topology
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+affine+space"} locally affine space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+site"} Zariski site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi QCoh
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QCoh_X
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaves
            on a scheme
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\subset X
            correspond to especially nice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            functors
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Q
                      %mi Y
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q^*_Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
            and which has its own right adjoint), second they are exact (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"}> flat
            ) and their right adjoint is also flat. Second the localizations pertaining to Zariski topology are of finite type (a fortiori, Zariski topology on an affine scheme is generated by the principal open subsets
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is expressed via the property that the family of localizations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_Y
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in the cover is a (jointly) conservative family (i.e. a morphism is invertible iff it is invertible in each element of a cover). Using the idea of Grothendieck which says, according to Manin, that
            = succeed "," do
              %em to do a geometry you do not need a space you only need an algebra of functions on this would-be space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Proj
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can be reconstructed up to isomorphism from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi QCoh
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QCoh_X
          %h2#formal_frameworks Formal frameworks
          %p
            The above ideas are sometimes loosely expressed by practioners to take an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            as a noncommutative space (Smith calls it a
            %em quasischeme
            in that case). Some prefer to have a pair of an abelian category and a distinguished object in it. The work of Kontsevich–Rosenberg on noncommutative smooth spaces notices that the main noncommutative examples (those close to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
            algebras, rather than almost commutative quantum, PI and similar examples) like noncommutative projective spaces and noncommutative grassmanians (unlike quantum projective spaces and quantum flag varieties) are
            %em not
            locally affine with respect to the covers by biflat affine localizations, but rather locally affine in certain noncommutative smooth topologies (covers by Cohn localizations, which are not biflat, also appear occasionally). For the projective case, F. van Oystaeyen prefers to work with Ore localizations only and defines an interesting class of so-called
            %strong schematic algebras
            and axiomatizes a more general property of torsion lattices which can play a role in an extension of quasicoherent sheaf theory to similar situations (“noncommutative topology”). T. Maszczyk prefers to generalize categories of quasicoherent sheaves to monoidal abelian categories and requires that the corresponding direct or inverse image functors respect the monoidal structure in a lax or colax way. That means that the commutative modules are viewed as
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+bimodule"} central bimodule
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spans"} spans
          %p
            Rosenberg notices that the considerations from the Idea section force one to consider not noncommutative schemes in an absolute sense, but rather noncommutative schemes relative to the ground category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi QCoh
                      %mrow
                        %mi Spec
                        %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = QCoh_{Spec k}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive
            (there is a more subtle variant in the nonabelian case). Then the general nonsense basically forces one to a unique notion of a noncommutative scheme.
          %h2#rosenbergs_definition Rosenberg’s definition
          %p
            (A. Rosenberg,
            = succeed "," do
              %em Noncommutative schemes
            %strong 112
            (1998) 93–125,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1000479824211"} doi
          %p
            A quasicompact
            %strong relative noncommutative scheme
            over a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint pair
            of functors (direct and inverse image)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_* : A\to C : q^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Q
                    %mi λ
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{Q^*_\lambda : A\to A_\lambda\}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}
            whose direct image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{\lambda *}
            and inverse image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Q
                    %mi λ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_\lambda^*
            parts are exact and such that the composition of the direct image functor of each localization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi λ
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{\lambda*} : A_\lambda\to A
            followed by the direct image functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_* : A\to C
            is also affine.
          %p (A pair of adjoint functors, inverse and direct image represent an affine morphism if the direct image is faithtful and it has its own right adjoint, so we have an adjoint triple with middle term, that is the direct image, faithful. For the affine localizations, the direct image is in addition full.)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The word ‘localization’ here is in the sense of Gabriel–Zisman. Notice that if the ground category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has a distinguished object (say the generator in the category of vector spaces over a field) then its direct image functor is a distinguished object in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/zoranskoda/show/gluing+categories+from+localizations"} gluing categories from localizations
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Quantum projective spaces, quantum flag varieties, commutative quasicompact schemes, the
            = succeed "-schemes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %h2#nonexamples Nonexamples
          %p Noncommutative projective spaces, noncommutative grassmannians, universal noncommutative flag variety.
